+/*
+ * SWEET (R86): offline-derived rebase tables for the two executables the
+ * kernel hand-maps (dyld and launchd).
+ *
+ * F142 (R85): the UFAULT at dyld+0x33a9c "br x4" with lr dyld+0x3656c
+ * comes from dyld+0x33a94 "ldr x4,[x8,#0x38]", where x8 = ctx->vtable
+ * = dyld+0x80060.  The file's pointer slots are DYLD_CHAINED_PTR_64-
+ * encoded rebase entries (stride in bits 52-55 in 8-byte units, target
+ * in bits 0-48; verified offline: chains cover every entry with zero
+ * overlap), but dyld.bin has NO LC_DYLD_CHAINED_FIXUPS, its LC_DYLD_INFO
+ * is garbage (rebase_off=0 pointing at the mach header), and dyld has no
+ * self-fixup code.  Nobody applies the rebases, so every slot is either
+ * the raw encoded entry or -- for pages whose disk read came back
+ * all-zero (F138/F139) -- zero.  The allocator then calls vtable slot 7
+ * (file 0x80098, target = the null-import stub dyld+0x3607c "ret")
+ * through a NULL pointer: pc=0.
+ *
+ * Fix: the kernel applies the rebases itself right after the fill, from
+ * the (file offset, target) pairs below, derived offline from the
+ * on-disk binaries.  Writes go through the physmap alias of the wired
+ * page (the R81-proven delivery path) and are mirrored into resident
+ * object pages via sweet_objfill() so a later fault takeover cannot
+ * resurrect the encoded entries.
+ *
+ * Generated offline; regenerate from tools/gen-rebase-tables.py.
+ */
